Storm season is here. The first two named storms of the **2026 to 2027** season, **Storm Austen** and **Storm Boelo**, arrived in the same week in late October, bringing strong winds and heavy rain. More storms are likely over the winter.

In the same week, the government launched **Be Ready**, a campaign asking every household to take a few simple steps to prepare for emergencies such as severe weather and power cuts.

For many disabled people, a storm is not just an inconvenience. If you rely on **powered medical equipment**, a **stairlift**, an **electric wheelchair**, a **telecare alarm** or **medicines that need a fridge**, a power cut can quickly become serious. This guide explains what Be Ready asks you to do, and the free help you can get.

{% callout type="danger" title="In an emergency" %}
If someone's life is in danger, call **999**. If you are deaf or cannot speak, you can register for **emergencySMS** so you can text 999 (see below). For urgent medical help that is not an emergency, call **NHS 111**. In a power cut, call **105**.
{% /callout %}

## What is Be Ready?

**Be Ready** was launched by the **Cabinet Office** on **22 October 2026**. It is the first part of a **three year** campaign to help households feel more prepared for events like storms, floods and loss of power, water, phone or internet.

The idea is simple. If households that can prepare do so, **emergency services can focus on the people who need them most**. That is why it matters for disabled people. Planning ahead means you are less likely to be left without help, and the services you rely on can reach you faster.

## The Be Ready checklist

The government suggests keeping enough to manage at home for **3 days**:

- **water:** 3 litres per person per day, for 3 days
- **food** that does not need a fridge or cooking, for 3 days
- a **torch** and spare batteries
- a **battery or wind-up radio**
- a charged **power bank** for your phone
- some **cash**, in case card payments are down
- **copies of important documents**
- a **written list** of important phone numbers and **your medicines**, in case your phone is not working
- a **first aid kit**
- **blankets** and warm clothing

It is advice, not a rule. You do not need to buy everything at once. You can build it up slowly during your normal shopping.

{% callout type="info" title="Add your own needs to the list" %}
The standard checklist is a starting point. Think about what **you** need to stay safe and well for 3 days. For example:

- enough **medicines** to last, and your **repeat prescription** details
- spare **batteries or chargers** for hearing aids, mobility aids and communication devices
- **continence products**, dressings or other supplies
- food you can **eat safely**, if you have allergies, swallowing problems or a special diet
- food and supplies for an **assistance dog**
- anything that helps you feel calm, such as ear defenders or a familiar object
{% /callout %}

## Join the Priority Services Register

The **Priority Services Register (PSR)** is one of the most useful things you can do, and it is **free**. Be Ready asks everyone who is eligible to sign up.

If you are on the register, your energy and water companies know you may need **extra help**. Depending on your needs, they can:

- give you **advance warning** of planned power cuts
- give you **priority support** and updates during a power cut, especially if you rely on medical equipment
- arrange **bottled water** to be delivered if your water supply is cut off
- set up a **password scheme**, so you know a caller is genuine
- send information in **large print, Braille or other formats**
- let a **trusted person** be contacted on your behalf

### Who can join?

You can usually join if you, or someone you live with:

- is **disabled** or has a **long-term health condition**
- has a **mental health condition**
- has **reduced mobility**
- relies on **medical equipment** that needs power, or needs **water** for medical reasons
- has a **sight or hearing** impairment
- is of **State Pension age**
- is **pregnant** or has a **child under 5**
- needs extra help for a short time, for example after an operation

### How to join

Energy and water companies keep **separate** registers, so it is worth signing up with all of them:

1. **Your energy supplier.** This is the company that sends your gas or electricity bill. Ask by phone or on their website.
2. **Your electricity network operator.** This is the company that looks after the cables and fixes power cuts in your area. The **thepsr.co.uk** website can tell you who yours is and help you sign up.
3. **Your water company.** Ask them to add you to their priority services list.

Some companies share details with each other if you agree, but always check you are on each list. It only takes a few minutes, and you can ask a family member, carer or support worker to do it with you.

See more about [help with energy and water bills](/support/money-off-bills/energy-and-water/).

## If you rely on medical equipment

If you use equipment such as a **home oxygen concentrator**, a **ventilator**, a **suction machine**, a **dialysis machine**, a **hoist**, a **pressure-relieving mattress** or an **electric bed**:

- **tell your electricity network operator** and make sure you are on the PSR
- **ask your equipment provider** what to do in a power cut, and how long any battery lasts
- if you use **home oxygen**, ask your oxygen supplier about **back-up cylinders**
- keep **batteries charged**, including for electric wheelchairs, scooters and stairlifts, when a storm is forecast
- write down **who to call**, and keep the list somewhere easy to find

{% callout type="warn" title="Never" %}
Never use a **barbecue, camping stove or petrol generator indoors**. They can cause deadly **carbon monoxide** poisoning. Use torches rather than candles if you can, to reduce the risk of fire.
{% /callout %}

## Medicines

- **Order repeat prescriptions** in good time, especially if a storm is forecast or over Christmas.
- Keep a **written list** of your medicines and doses.
- If you have medicines that must be kept in a **fridge**, such as some insulins, ask your pharmacist what to do if the power goes off. Keep the fridge door shut as much as you can.
- If you run out or cannot get to the pharmacy, call **NHS 111**.

## Phones, alarms and staying in touch

Most landlines are moving to **digital phone lines** that run through your broadband router. These **stop working in a power cut** unless you have a battery back-up.

- If you rely on your landline, for example because you have **no mobile signal** or use a **telecare or personal alarm**, tell your phone provider. They should offer you a **free solution**, such as a battery back-up, so you can call 999 for at least an hour in a power cut.
- Tell your **telecare provider** too, so they can check your alarm will still work.
- Keep a **charged power bank** for your mobile phone.

### emergencySMS

If you are **deaf, hard of hearing** or have a **speech impairment**, you can register for **emergencySMS**. It lets you contact the police, ambulance, fire service or coastguard by **text message**. You need to **register before** you need it: text the word **register** to **999**, and follow the instructions.

## Make a plan with someone you trust

Be Ready suggests agreeing a **back-up plan** with anyone who may need extra help, especially if they live alone. For example:

- agree that if the power goes off and you cannot call, a **family member, friend or neighbour will come round** in person
- give a trusted person a **spare key**, or tell them where to find one
- make sure your **carer or care agency** knows your plan, and what to do if they cannot reach you
- if you would need help to **leave your home**, for example in a flood, think now about how you would do it and who could help

## When a storm is forecast

The **Met Office** names storms when they could cause medium or high impacts. Check the forecast and weather warnings, which come as **yellow**, **amber** or **red**.

- **Charge** your phone, power bank, wheelchair and other devices
- **Bring in or tie down** things outside that could blow away, like bins and garden furniture
- **Stay indoors** if you can during the worst of the weather
- **Avoid travelling** unless you must. Check whether transport, appointments or care visits are affected
- **Keep away from the coast**, rivers and fallen power lines. Never touch a power line, and call **105** to report one

### Flooding

Storms can bring flooding. You can **sign up for free flood warnings** by phone, text or email on GOV.UK, or call **Floodline** on **0345 988 1188**. In Wales, Natural Resources Wales runs the same service.

## Help with costs after a storm

- **Home and contents insurance** may cover storm damage. Contact your insurer as soon as you can, and take photos.
- If you are on a low income and face an emergency, your council may be able to help. In England, ask about the **Crisis and Resilience Fund**. In Wales, you can apply to the **Discretionary Assistance Fund**.
- Read our guide to [disability and winter](/articles/disability-and-surviving-winter/) for help with energy bills and keeping warm.

{% callout type="danger" title="If you are struggling" %}
Storms and emergencies can be frightening, especially if you live alone. If you are finding it hard to cope, call **Samaritans** free, any time, on **116 123**. If you or someone else is in danger, call **999**.
{% /callout %}
